Well, it's all about equilibrium and how
it can shift from one way to another.

00:14:28.190 --> 00:14:31.160
The goal is to keep the
reaction in the middle.

00:14:31.600 --> 00:14:38.190
So if all of a sudden I decide to increase
the amount of base in the solution, well,

00:14:38.190 --> 00:14:40.830
what is the buffer going to exactly do?

00:14:41.240 --> 00:14:46.140
Well, when we increase that, it's
going to try to absorb it, right?

00:14:46.140 --> 00:14:49.310
It's going to try to keep
it into the center here.

00:14:49.689 --> 00:14:54.959
Now, so when you add more base to the
solution, it has to respond to that.

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:56.880
Is it going to let it become basic?

00:14:57.160 --> 00:15:00.239
Well, no, or the entire
system is going to fall apart.

00:15:00.670 --> 00:15:04.959
What's going to happen then is being that
this whole reaction here has this double

00:15:05.020 --> 00:15:06.869
arrow that we've talked about before.

00:15:07.370 --> 00:15:08.800
Well, the reaction can either happen.

00:15:09.189 --> 00:15:12.449
in the forward direction
or in the reverse.

00:15:12.850 --> 00:15:17.830
So if all of a sudden I decide to
increase the basic side, which is the

00:15:17.840 --> 00:15:23.530
A minus, well, what is going to happen
to, to essentially to change this?

00:15:24.005 --> 00:15:27.805
Well, the system is going to respond
and instead it's going to actually

00:15:27.814 --> 00:15:30.295
try to shift the reaction backwards.

00:15:30.444 --> 00:15:34.205
It's like oh There's too much
product laying around So in order to

00:15:34.205 --> 00:15:38.135
change the scales of this reaction
and how everything is balanced,

00:15:38.454 --> 00:15:40.105
it's going to shift it back back.

00:15:40.485 --> 00:15:44.805
So when all of a sudden you increase
a lot of base here, you're going

00:15:44.805 --> 00:15:48.555
to now see that the reaction is
going to shift back to the center.

00:15:48.755 --> 00:15:50.425
It's going to try to hold it there.

00:15:50.535 --> 00:15:51.564
And that's what a buffer does.

00:15:51.564 --> 00:15:55.145
It neutralizes all these different
components as being added to you.

00:15:55.454 --> 00:15:58.624
Like your blood or your circulatory
system, when you introduce something

00:15:58.624 --> 00:16:02.844
that's basic to your body or acidic, your
body is trying to maintain its levels

00:16:02.844 --> 00:16:04.985
so that it can operate appropriately.

00:16:05.255 --> 00:16:06.954
It has to be kept in the middle.

00:16:07.364 --> 00:16:11.124
So if I do the opposite, where all of
a sudden I'm like, well, I want to now,

00:16:11.504 --> 00:16:13.545
uh, have all these acids in my body.

00:16:13.545 --> 00:16:17.744
So let's say you drink a bunch
of soda or you, uh, have a bunch

00:16:17.744 --> 00:16:19.104
of energy drinks or coffee.

00:16:19.244 --> 00:16:20.545
Well, what's your body going to do?

00:16:20.545 --> 00:16:24.244
Is it just going to let it become acidic
and allow it to kind of poison itself?

00:16:24.245 --> 00:16:24.264
And yeah.

00:16:24.455 --> 00:16:26.275
No, it's going to, it's
going to fight back.

00:16:26.525 --> 00:16:30.715
So when you have something that's very
acidic being added to your body, well,

00:16:30.715 --> 00:16:35.665
instead of it shifting, uh, in the
reverse direction, like we did before,

00:16:36.034 --> 00:16:40.464
it's now going to cause it to shift
forward in order to neutralize itself.

00:16:40.864 --> 00:16:45.425
So being that when we increase a
ton of acid, it's now going to shift

00:16:46.065 --> 00:16:48.345
forward to respond to that change.

00:16:48.665 --> 00:16:51.225
It's all about, is there a response?

00:16:51.255 --> 00:16:53.695
And that's how the idea
of buffers, they respond.

00:16:54.045 --> 00:16:56.385
And resist change of pH.

00:16:56.750 --> 00:17:00.250
Let's try to look at this one basic
calculation to finish this video.

00:17:00.400 --> 00:17:05.120
So when it comes to actually trying to
analyze where does your buffer work, this

00:17:05.120 --> 00:17:10.689
is all about understanding what is the Ka
value and actually eventually deriving an

00:17:10.689 --> 00:17:14.890
equation that can be used to be able to
determine how well does your buffer work.

00:17:15.269 --> 00:17:20.380
So if I had the following, it says
that the Ka for acetic acid Uh, is 1.

00:17:20.420 --> 00:17:22.260
8 times 10 to the negative 5.

00:17:22.819 --> 00:17:29.160
What is the pH of the buffer, uh, that
is prepared with 1 molar acetic acid?

00:17:29.530 --> 00:17:33.810
And then one molar of acetate
ion, which is the conjugate base.

00:17:34.330 --> 00:17:37.690
So if I were to do that, how
would the entire thing work?

00:17:37.690 --> 00:17:38.960
How would we set this up?

00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:39.210
Right?

00:17:39.210 --> 00:17:41.380
We never talked about
something like this before.

00:17:42.030 --> 00:17:46.350
Well, the one thing we can do is we can
set up a Ka equation in that we have a Ka.

00:17:46.799 --> 00:17:50.460
So what we will first do is,
well, if you remember, is that Ka.

00:17:50.890 --> 00:17:55.330
so is equal to the products over
the reactants of your formula.

00:17:55.890 --> 00:17:59.939
So if we have our Ka, what we
will do is put our products, which

00:17:59.939 --> 00:18:07.179
in this case is H3O, uh, or H3O
plus, and we have our acetic acid.

00:18:07.179 --> 00:18:10.939
And remember, all these are
referred to in brackets as well.

00:18:11.629 --> 00:18:15.310
So we have our products
all over the reactants.

00:18:16.430 --> 00:18:18.880
And then we'll take that expression.

00:18:19.470 --> 00:18:24.480
Now, from here, what we can essentially
do is we can kind of rearrange it to

00:18:24.480 --> 00:18:27.820
actually solve for what is the pH.

00:18:28.190 --> 00:18:30.030
Well, so exactly how do you do that?

00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:34.000
Well, if you kind of remember
from your equations, is that pH

00:18:34.019 --> 00:18:40.760
is equal to the negative log of
OH, of negative log of H3O plus.

00:18:41.300 --> 00:18:46.650
So what we have to actually do is
rearrange this formula to get H3O plus.

00:18:47.014 --> 00:18:47.824
By itself.

00:18:47.834 --> 00:18:49.584
So can we do that?

00:18:49.584 --> 00:18:53.525
Well, if we kind of do some mathematics,
just to sort of cancel things out.

00:18:53.825 --> 00:18:57.635
So for example, if I were to look
at this and I were to, let's say,

00:18:57.665 --> 00:19:03.645
multiply both sides, by the acid
and base, so to cancel things out.

00:19:03.645 --> 00:19:04.995
So let's kind of move things around.

00:19:05.185 --> 00:19:10.134
So what I'm doing here is I essentially
am multiplying by the opposites, so

00:19:10.134 --> 00:19:12.534
that way everything cancels our units.

00:19:12.534 --> 00:19:15.504
So as we can see, the
conjugate base cancels.

00:19:16.074 --> 00:19:17.205
acid cancels.

00:19:17.415 --> 00:19:20.365
So we're multiplying and
dividing by the different sides.

00:19:20.814 --> 00:19:25.314
So when we do that, we now have
on the right hand side, the Ka

00:19:25.684 --> 00:19:30.824
multiplied by the conjugate, by
the acid and the conjugate base.

00:19:30.824 --> 00:19:32.504
So both those concentrations.

00:19:32.934 --> 00:19:36.675
So we're sort of rearranging
this equation, in order

00:19:36.675 --> 00:19:40.125
to get H3O plus by itself.

00:19:40.665 --> 00:19:45.865
Now the whole reason we're doing this is
so that way we can actually find the pH.

00:19:46.275 --> 00:19:49.784
Now the last step is all about well
in order to find pH you have to

00:19:49.784 --> 00:19:51.924
take the negative log of everything.

00:19:52.405 --> 00:19:57.155
So if we kind of do do that take
the negative log of our H3O plus

00:19:57.155 --> 00:20:06.084
concentration we would have pH is equal
to the negative log of Ka times the

00:20:06.085 --> 00:20:08.910
negative log Of our concentrations.

00:20:09.764 --> 00:20:12.165
So now, why is this something
that's so important?

00:20:12.175 --> 00:20:17.105
Well, this is essentially how we define
the Henderson Hasselbalch equation,

00:20:17.465 --> 00:20:19.515
which is quite the complicated name.

00:20:19.975 --> 00:20:25.804
So now, what this whole idea is, by
deriving this, this, this Ka with

00:20:25.805 --> 00:20:29.645
a negative log is actually known
as something known as the pKa.

00:20:30.035 --> 00:20:33.455
So what we do here is we take
that negative log of the Ka.

00:20:33.740 --> 00:20:36.690
negative log of our,
of our acids and bases.

00:20:36.990 --> 00:20:40.340
And it actually, we can plug all
of our different components in

00:20:40.340 --> 00:20:41.740
that we started with the problem.

00:20:42.409 --> 00:20:48.019
So if I take my pH is equal to
negative log of our K a value,

00:20:48.019 --> 00:20:50.340
which from earlier was 1.

00:20:50.379 --> 00:20:52.270
8 times 10 to the negative five.

00:20:53.490 --> 00:20:57.410
And then essentially we multiply
that by the negative log of our

00:20:57.410 --> 00:21:00.730
concentrations, which were one over one.

00:21:01.229 --> 00:21:02.269
So it was one mole.

00:21:02.850 --> 00:21:06.300
Divided by one mole, and the whole
thing is that number over there

00:21:06.300 --> 00:21:08.250
is going to end up cancelling out.

00:21:08.990 --> 00:21:11.810
And once we put all this
into our calculator by

00:21:11.830 --> 00:21:13.800
taking the negative log of 1.

00:21:13.800 --> 00:21:17.340
8 times 10 to the negative 5th, times
it by the negative log of 1 divided

00:21:17.340 --> 00:21:21.939
by 1, what we are going to get is
the pH of where this buffer works.

00:21:22.450 --> 00:21:27.629
Which, after we place that into your
calculator, you're going to get a pH of 4.

00:21:27.710 --> 00:21:28.240
74.

00:21:28.745 --> 00:21:30.745
is where this buffer works.

00:21:30.745 --> 00:21:34.764
So if you had acetic acid as
a buffer, it would be able to

00:21:34.764 --> 00:21:36.615
maintain a pH right around 4.

00:21:36.615 --> 00:21:40.815
74, which the kind of let you
know, it's like to go back up

00:21:40.815 --> 00:21:42.894
to the example I had earlier.

00:21:43.095 --> 00:21:47.124
So earlier I talked about, well,
if the concentrations were equal to

00:21:47.125 --> 00:21:50.465
each other, which is what we had,
well then essentially the buffer

00:21:50.465 --> 00:21:53.775
would be able to hold right around 4.

00:21:53.775 --> 00:21:53.794
74.

00:21:54.175 --> 00:21:58.355
So it could either increase the
pH by one or decrease it by one.

00:21:58.645 --> 00:22:04.925
So within our buffer capacity, it
could hold the pH from anywhere from 3.

00:22:04.925 --> 00:22:05.825
74 all the way to 5.

00:22:05.825 --> 00:22:09.195
74 in order to maintain
it within a pH of 2.

00:22:09.605 --> 00:22:14.554
Now this is very critically important
for biological systems which have enzymes

00:22:14.554 --> 00:22:20.185
and proteins um, that essentially are
very sensitive to the pH without them

00:22:20.185 --> 00:22:22.035
being in their very specific ranges.

00:22:22.350 --> 00:22:25.540
They will denature, they will
cease to function correctly, they

00:22:25.540 --> 00:22:27.669
won't be able to react properly.

00:22:27.969 --> 00:22:30.259
So this is the general idea
of things that you'll see in

00:22:30.259 --> 00:22:32.409
physiology and in future courses.

00:22:33.590 --> 00:22:36.899
This is the idea with buffers, uh,
